# sites.conf — the site registry. Copy to <websites-root>/sites.conf (default ~/websites/sites.conf).
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE LIVES ON THE PLATFORM SIDE, NEVER IN A SITE'S REPO. That is the whole point.
# deploy-site.sh resolves the repo it is run from and looks that repo up HERE to decide which
# site it may publish. A site's own session therefore cannot publish another site: there is no
# site argument to misuse, and nothing it edits inside its own repo changes the answer, because
# this map is what is consulted. Adding a row is a platform change, made by whoever owns the
# platform (guide/examples/E17-public-websites.md § 7).
#
# One site per line, pipe-separated; blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored:
#
#   name | repo path | build command | output dir
#
# name           lowercase letters, digits and hyphens; must not end in "-stg". It names the
#                serving roots: <websites-root>/srv/<name>/ and <websites-root>/srv/<name>-stg/.
# repo path      the site's git repo; matched by its real path, so a symlinked path still matches.
#                A leading ~ is expanded. Register each repo once: a repo listed twice is refused.
# build command  run from the repo root; `-` for a site that needs no build. It cannot contain `|`
#                (the separator): put a longer build in a script and call that.
# output dir     relative to the repo root, inside it (no ..): what gets copied to the serving root.
#
# Adding a row is not the whole of adding a site. Provision its serving root, its proxy block,
# its monitoring endpoint and its backup in the same change — a site that is trivial to add and
# unmonitored or unbacked is a liability, not a feature.

<site-name> | ~/<path-to>/<site-repo> | npm run build | dist
